      "🔥 Day 6: Types of Dependency Injection (Constructor vs Setter vs Field Injection)\n\nDependency Injection (DI) is the heart of Spring Framework ❤️\nSpring supports multiple ways to inject dependencies into a class.\nLet's understand the 3 main types 👇\n\n🔹 What is Dependency Injection?\n👉 Dependency Injection means providing required objects (dependencies) from outside instead of creating them inside the class.\nExample:\nCar needs Engine\nSpring creates the Engine and injects it into Car.\n\n🔥 Real-Life Analogy\nConstructor Injection\n🚗 Buy a car with engine already installed.\nSetter Injection\n🚗 Buy car first, install engine later.\nField Injection\n🚗 Mechanic secretly installs engine inside car.\nYou don't see how it happened 😄\n\n🔹 Which One Should You Use?\n✅ Constructor Injection → Preferred\n✅ Setter Injection → Optional dependencies\n❌ Field Injection → Avoid in production code\n\n🔥 Interview Question\nQ: Which Dependency Injection type is recommended in Spring Boot?\n✅ Answer:\nConstructor Injection is recommended because it promotes immutability, easier testing, and clearer dependency management.\n\n💡 Pro Tip:\nIn Spring Boot 4+, Constructor Injection is the industry standard and preferred approach 🚀\n\n📌 Final Thought:\n\"Constructor Injection creates cleaner, safer, and more maintainable Spring applications.\"",
