    description:
      '🔥 Day 7: IoC vs DI (Clear Difference with Examples)\n\nMany Spring beginners get confused between IoC (Inversion of Control) and DI (Dependency Injection) 🤔\nLet\'s understand them in the simplest way possible 👇\n\n🔹 What is IoC?\n👉 Inversion of Control is a design principle where the control of object creation and management is transferred from the application to the Spring Container.\nWithout IoC:\nEngine engine = new Engine();\n👉 You create and manage the object yourself.\nWith IoC:\n@Component\nclass Engine {\n}\n👉 Spring creates and manages the object.\n\n🔥 What is DI?\n👉 Dependency Injection is a technique used to implement IoC.\nInstead of creating dependencies inside a class:\n\nclass Car {\n private Engine engine =\n new Engine();\n}\nSpring injects them:\n@Component\nclass Car {\n\n private final Engine engine;\n\n public Car(Engine engine) {\n this.engine = engine;\n }\n}\n\n👉 Spring automatically provides the Engine object.\n🔹 Real-World Analogy \nIoC\n☕ Restaurant manages cooking.\nYou don\'t enter the kitchen and cook yourself.\nThe restaurant controls the process.\n\nDI\n☕ The waiter brings coffee to your table.\nYou don\'t prepare it yourself.\nThe dependency (coffee) is injected into your hands 😄\n\n🔹 Relationship Between IoC & DI\nIoC\n ↓\nImplemented Using\n ↓\nDependency Injection\n👉 IoC = Concept\n👉 DI = Implementation\n\n🔹 Spring Internally\nSpring Container\n ↓\nCreates Engine Bean\n ↓\nCreates Car Bean\n ↓\nInjects Engine into Car\n👉 Creation = IoC\n👉 Injection = DI\n\n🔥 Interview Question\nQ: Is IoC the same as DI?\n✅ Answer:\nNo.\nIoC is a design principle where Spring controls object creation and lifecycle.\nDI is a technique used by Spring to provide dependencies to objects and achieve IoC.\n\n🔹 Easy Way to Remember\n👉 IoC = Who controls objects?\nAnswer: Spring.\n👉 DI = How dependencies are provided?\nAnswer: Spring injects them.\n\n💡 Pro Tip:\nWhenever someone asks:\n"How does Spring achieve IoC?"\n✅ Correct Answer:\n"Spring achieves IoC using Dependency Injection."\n\n📌 Final Thought:\n"IoC gives control to Spring, and DI is the mechanism Spring uses to provide dependencies."',
