    image: "public/Spring/Day_9.png",
    description:
      '🔥 Day 9: Bean – What & Why?\n\nOne of the most fundamental concepts in Spring Framework 🌱\nIf you\'re learning Spring, understanding Beans is a must because almost everything revolves around them.\n\n🔹 What is a Bean?\n👉 Definition: A Bean is an object that is created, configured, and managed by the Spring Container.\nSimply put:\nSpring Managed Object = Bean\n\n🔥 Normal Java Object vs Spring Bean\nNormal Java Object\nEngine engine = new Engine();\n👉 You create and manage the object manually.\n\nSpring Bean\n@Component\nclass Engine {\n}\n👉 Spring creates and manages the object automatically.\n\n🔹 Why Do We Need Beans?\nWithout Spring Beans:\n❌ Manual object creation\n❌ Tight coupling\n❌ Difficult dependency management\n❌ More boilerplate code\nWith Spring Beans:\n✅ Automatic object creation\n✅ Dependency Injection\n✅ Better maintainability\n✅ Loose coupling\n\n🔥 Creating a Bean Using @Component\nimport org.springframework.stereotype.Component;\n@Component\nclass Engine {\n public void start() {\n System.out.println("Engine Started");\n }\n}\n\n🔹 Spring Automatically Does\nApplication Starts\n ↓\nSpring Scans Components\n ↓\nCreates Engine Bean\n ↓\nStores Bean in Container\n\n🔥 Using Bean in Another Class\n@Component\nclass Car {\n private final Engine engine;\n\n public Car(Engine engine) {\n this.engine = engine;\n }\n\n public void drive() {\n engine.start();\n }\n}\n\n🔹 What Happens Internally?\nSpring Container\n ↓\nCreates Engine Bean\n ↓\nCreates Car Bean\n ↓\nInjects Engine into Car\n\n🔥 Other Ways to Create Beans\nUsing @Bean Annotation\n@Configuration\nclass AppConfig {\n @Bean\n public Engine engine() {\n return new Engine();\n }\n}\n👉 Spring registers Engine as a Bean.\n\n🔹 Bean Lifecycle (Simple View)\nCreate Bean\n ↓\nInject Dependencies\n ↓\nReady to Use\n ↓\nDestroy Bean\n\n🔥 Real-Life Analogy\n🏭 Factory\nWithout Bean:\nYou build every machine manually.\nWith Bean:\nFactory creates and manages machines for you.\nYou simply use them.\n\n🔥 Benefits of Beans\n✔ Automatic object creation\n✔ Dependency management\n✔ Easy testing\n✔ Better scalability\n✔ Cleaner architecture\n\n🔥 Interview Question\nQ: What is a Spring Bean?\n✅ Answer:\n"A Spring Bean is an object that is instantiated, configured, and managed by the Spring Container."\n\n💡 Pro Tip:\nWhenever you see @Component, @Service, @Repository, or @Controller, Spring creates a Bean behind the scenes 🚀\n\n📌 Final Thought:\n"Beans are the building blocks of every Spring application."',
