      '🔥 Day 10: How Spring Creates and Manages Beans\n\nOne of the most important Spring Framework concepts 🚀\nMany developers use Spring Beans daily, but few understand what happens behind the scenes.\nLet\'s see how Spring creates and manages Beans 👇\n\n🔹 Quick Recap: What is a Bean?\n👉 A Bean is an object that is created, configured, and managed by the Spring Container.\nExample:\n@Component\nclass Engine {\n}\nHere, Engine becomes a Spring Bean.\n\n🔥 Step 1: Application Starts\n🔥 Step 2: Component Scanning Spring scans packages for:\n🔥 Step 3: Bean Creation\n🔥 Step 4: Store Bean in Container\n🔥 Step 5: Dependency Injection\n🔥 Step 6: Bean Ready to Use\n\n🔥 Bean Lifecycle (Simplified)\nApplication Start\n ↓\nComponent Scan\n ↓\nBean Creation\n ↓\nDependency Injection\n ↓\nBean Initialization\n ↓\nReady to Use\n ↓\nApplication Shutdown\n ↓\nBean Destruction\n\nSpring creates both automatically.\n\n🔹 Real-Life Analogy\n🏭 Factory Manager\nWithout Spring:\nYou hire workers and assign tasks manually.\nWith Spring:\nManager automatically hires workers, assigns jobs, and manages everything.\n\n👉 Spring Container = Factory Manager\n\n🔥 Why Spring Bean Management Matters?\n✔ Loose Coupling\n✔ Automatic Dependency Injection\n✔ Easier Testing\n✔ Better Scalability\n✔ Cleaner Code\n\n🔥 Interview Question\nQ: How does Spring create Beans?\n✅ Answer:\n"Spring scans classes using component scanning, creates bean objects, stores them in the IoC Container, injects dependencies, and manages their complete lifecycle."\n\n💡 Pro Tip:\nWhenever Spring sees @Component, @Service, @Repository, or @Controller, it automatically creates and manages a Bean behind the scenes 🚀\n\n📌 Final Thought:\n"Spring Container acts like a smart manager—it creates, stores, injects, and manages Beans throughout the application\'s lifecycle."',
