    image: "public/Spring/Day_12.png",
    description:
      '🔥 Day 12: Bean Scopes in Spring (Singleton vs Prototype)\n\nBy default, Spring creates and manages Beans as Singletons. But did you know Spring supports multiple bean scopes? 🤔\nUnderstanding Bean Scopes is important for Spring interviews and real-world applications 🚀\n\n🔹 What is Bean Scope?\n👉 Bean Scope defines how many bean objects Spring should create and manage.\nIn simple words:\nBean Scope\n ↓\nObject Creation Strategy\n\n🔥 Why Do We Need Bean Scopes?\nDifferent applications require different object lifecycles.\nExamples:\n✔ Database Service → One shared object\n✔ Shopping Cart → Separate object per user\nSpring solves this using Bean Scopes.\n\n🔥 1. Singleton Scope (Default)\n👉 Spring creates only ONE object for the entire application.\n\n🔥 2. Prototype Scope\n👉 Spring creates a new object every time the bean is requested.\n\n👉 Different objects created.\n\n🔹 Real-Life Analogy\n☕ Coffee Order\n\nEvery time you order coffee:\n☕ New cup created\n👉 Prototype Scope\n\n🔥 Visual Comparison\nSingleton\nRequest 1\n ↓\n Engine #1\n\nRequest 2\n ↓\n Engine #1\n\nRequest 3\n ↓\n Engine #1\n\nSame object reused.\n\nPrototype\nRequest 1\n ↓\n Engine #1\n\nRequest 2\n ↓\n Engine #2\n\nRequest 3\n ↓\n Engine #3\n\nNew object each time.\n\n🔹 Common Singleton Beans\n✔ Service Classes\n✔ Repository Classes\n✔ Configuration Classes\n✔ Utility Components\n\n🔹 Common Prototype Beans\n✔ Shopping Cart\n✔ Report Generator\n✔ User Session Objects\n✔ Temporary Processing Objects\n\n🔥 Interview Question\nQ: What is the default scope of a Spring Bean?\n✅ Answer:\nThe default scope of a Spring Bean is Singleton, meaning Spring creates only one instance per container.\n\n🔥 Quick Summary\nSingleton\n ↓\nOne Object For Entire App\n\nPrototype\n ↓\nNew Object For Every Request\n\n💡 Pro Tip:\nAbout 90% of Spring Beans in real-world applications use Singleton scope 🚀\n\n📌 Final Thought:\n"Bean Scope determines whether Spring shares one object or creates multiple objects throughout the application lifecycle."',
