    description:
      "🔥 Day 60: Spring JDBC Mini Project – Student Management System\nCongratulations! 🎉\n\nWe've completed the fundamentals of Spring JDBC.\nNow it's time to build a real-world mini project that combines everything we've learned.\nLet's create a Student Management System using Spring JDBC. 🚀\n🔹 Project Overview\nOur application will perform basic CRUD operations.\n✅ Add Student\n✅ View Students\n✅ Update Student\n✅ Delete Student\n\n🔥 Project Architecture\nClient\n │\n ▼\nController\n │\n ▼\nService Layer\n │\n ▼\nRepository (DAO)\n │\n ▼\nJdbcTemplate\n │\n ▼\nDataSource\n │\n ▼\nMySQL Database\n\n🔥 Complete Application Flow\nUser Request\n ↓\nController\n ↓\nService\n ↓\nRepository\n ↓\nJdbcTemplate\n ↓\nDatabase\n ↓\nResponse Returned\n\n🔹 Real-Life Analogy\n🏫 College Office\nNew Admission\n ↓\nStore Student Record\n ↓\nSearch Student\n ↓\nUpdate Details\n ↓\nDelete Record\n\nJust like a college office manages student records, our Spring JDBC application performs CRUD operations on the database.\n🔥 Best Practices\n✅ Keep SQL queries in the Repository (DAO) layer.\n✅ Use JdbcTemplate instead of plain JDBC.\n✅ Use RowMapper for object mapping.\n✅ Use prepared statements (through JdbcTemplate) to help prevent SQL injection.\n✅ Separate Controller, Service, and Repository responsibilities.\n\n🔥 What We've Learned in Spring JDBC\n✅ DataSource\n✅ JdbcTemplate\n✅ RowMapper\n✅ CRUD Operations\n✅ Repository Layer\n✅ SQL Execution\nThese concepts form the foundation for building database-driven Spring applications.\n\n🔥 Interview Question\nQ: Which JdbcTemplate methods are commonly used for CRUD operations?\n✅ Answer:\nupdate() → Used for INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations.\nquery() → Retrieves multiple records.\nqueryForObject() → Retrieves a single record or value.\nThese methods simplify database access while handling resource management internally.\n\n🔹 Quick Summary\nStudent Management System\n │\n ▼\nController\n │\n ▼\nService\n │\n ▼\nRepository\n │\n ▼\nJdbcTemplate\n │\n ▼\nDatabase\n\n💡 Pro Tip:\nThis mini project demonstrates the complete Spring JDBC workflow and is an excellent project to discuss in interviews.\nRemember these key classes:\n✅ DataSource → Provides database connections.\n✅ JdbcTemplate → Executes SQL queries.\n✅ RowMapper → Converts rows into Java objects.\nMastering these three components will prepare you for both real-world development and Spring interviews.\n\n📌 Final Thought:\n\"Building a mini project is the best way to reinforce your Spring JDBC knowledge. By combining JdbcTemplate, RowMapper, and layered architecture, you can create clean, scalable, and maintainable database applications.\"",
