    title: "🚀 Day 132: Persistence Context in Spring Boot",
    image: "public/Spring/Day_132.png",
    description:
      "🔹 WHAT IS A PERSISTENCE CONTEXT?\nA Persistence Context is a first-level cache managed by the `EntityManager` that stores and tracks all managed entity instances during a transaction boundary.\n\nFlow: Java Objects ↕️ Persistence Context (1st Level Cache) ↕️ Database\n\n🔹 KEY RESPONSIBILITIES\n✅ First-Level Cache — Prevents duplicate database queries for the same entity ID within a single transaction.\n✅ Entity State Management — Tracks transitions between Transient, Managed, Detached, and Removed.\n✅ Dirty Checking — Detects modified fields on managed entities and auto-generates SQL updates at flush.\n✅ Identity Map — Guarantees that multiple calls to `find()` for the same primary key return the exact same Java object instance.\n\n🔹 FLUSH VS COMMIT\n📌 `flush()` — Synchronizes pending persistence context changes with the database (generates SQL), but does not commit the transaction.\n📌 `commit()` — Ends the database transaction, making flushed SQL changes permanent.\n\n🎯 INTERVIEW QUESTIONS\n1️⃣ What is the persistence context in JPA?\n👉 An internal working environment managed by `EntityManager` that tracks entity states and acts as a 1st-level cache.\n\n2️⃣ Is the first-level cache shared across multiple transactions?\n👉 No. The first-level cache is scoped exclusively to a single EntityManager/Persistence Context instance.",
