      "🚀 Day 139: File Upload APIs in Spring Boot | Build Secure REST APIs",
    image: "public/Spring/Day_139.png",
    description:
      '🔹 WHAT IS A FILE UPLOAD API?\nA file upload API allows clients to send files to a server through an HTTP request, typically using `multipart/form-data`. Spring Boot makes file uploads easy using Spring MVC and the `MultipartFile` interface.\n\nCommon use cases include profile picture uploads, resume and document management, image and video storage, CSV and Excel imports, and file attachments in applications.\n\n🔹 BASIC CONTROLLER EXAMPLE\n```java\n@PostMapping(value = "/upload", consumes = MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE)\npublic ResponseEntity<String> uploadFile(@RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) {\n    if (file.isEmpty()) return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("File is empty");\n    // Save file locally or upload to cloud storage (e.g., AWS S3)\n    return ResponseEntity.ok("File uploaded successfully: " + file.getOriginalFilename());\n}\n```\n\n🔹 CONFIGURING FILE SIZE LIMITS\nspring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size=10MB\nspring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size=10MB\n\n🔹 IMPORTANT SECURITY BEST PRACTICES\n🔐 Validate file size and actual file content (MIME types).\n🔐 Allow only supported file types.\n🔐 Generate server-side filenames instead of trusting user-supplied paths to prevent Path Traversal attacks (`../`).\n🔐 Store files outside the public web root directory.\n🔐 Add authentication and authorization where required.\n🔐 Scan potentially dangerous files for malware.\n🔐 Handle exceptions and log failures safely.\n🔐 Consider cloud object storage such as Amazon S3 for scalable production systems.\n\n🔹 COMMON INTERVIEW QUESTIONS & ANSWERS\n1️⃣ What is MultipartFile in Spring Boot?\n👉 An interface representing an uploaded file received in a multipart HTTP request, providing methods to access filename, content type, input stream, and transfer data.\n\n2️⃣ How do you upload a file using a REST API?\n👉 By creating a `@PostMapping` endpoint consuming `MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE` and accepting the file via `@RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file`.\n\n3️⃣ What is the difference between @RequestParam and @RequestPart?\n👉 `@RequestParam` is used for simple form fields and files; `@RequestPart` is used when complex JSON/XML metadata is passed alongside binary files in a multipart request.\n\n4️⃣ How do you configure the maximum file size?\n👉 By setting `spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size` and `spring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size` in `application.properties`.\n\n5️⃣ How can you validate uploaded files?\n👉 By checking file emptiness, enforcing maximum size limits, verifying MIME types and extensions against a strict allowlist, and inspecting file contents.\n\n6️⃣ How do you upload multiple files?\n👉 By accepting an array or `List<MultipartFile>` parameter in the controller method using `@RequestParam("files") List<MultipartFile> files`.\n\n7️⃣ How can you securely store and download files?\n👉 Store files outside the public web root using sanitized, server-generated filenames, and serve downloads through authenticated controllers returning `ResponseEntity<Resource>`.\n\n8️⃣ When should you use cloud storage instead of local storage?\n👉 Use cloud storage (such as AWS S3 or Google Cloud Storage) when scaling across multiple server instances (stateless architecture), managing massive file volumes, or requiring high availability and CDN integration.',
