    title:
      "🚀 Day 149: Security Configuration in Spring Boot | Secure Your APIs 🔐",
    image: "public/Spring/Day_149.png",
    description:
      '🔹 WHAT IS SECURITY CONFIGURATION?\nSecurity configuration defines how a Spring Boot application handles authentication, authorization, and HTTP security rules using a custom `SecurityFilterChain` bean annotated with `@EnableWebSecurity`.\n\n🔹 CONFIGURATION EXAMPLE\n```java\n@Configuration\n@EnableWebSecurity\npublic class SecurityConfig {\n    @Bean\n    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\n                .requestMatchers("/public/**").permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")\n                .anyRequest().authenticated()\n            )\n            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults())\n            .build();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n🔹 CORE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS & ANSWERS\n1️⃣ What is SecurityFilterChain?\n👉 A core Spring Security bean that defines a chain of security filters and authorization rules applied to matching HTTP requests.\n\n2️⃣ Why do we use @EnableWebSecurity?\n👉 It enables Spring Security\'s web security support and registers the necessary security configuration beans in the Spring application context.\n\n3️⃣ What is the difference between permitAll() and authenticated()?\n👉 `permitAll()` allows anonymous and authenticated users alike to access an endpoint without credentials; `authenticated()` requires the user to successfully log in first.\n\n4️⃣ What is the difference between hasRole() and hasAuthority()?\n👉 `hasRole()` automatically prepends the `ROLE_` prefix (evaluating against `ROLE_ADMIN`), whereas `hasAuthority()` checks exact permission strings without prefixing.\n\n5️⃣ Why is PasswordEncoder important?\n👉 It ensures user passwords are never stored in plain text by enforcing secure one-way cryptographic hashing (e.g., BCrypt).\n\n6️⃣ When should CSRF protection be enabled?\n👉 It should be enabled for applications using browser-based cookie authentication where session cookies are automatically included by the browser; it can be disabled for stateless APIs using token-based authentication (like JWT).\n\n7️⃣ What is the difference between 401 and 403 status codes?\n👉 HTTP 401 Unauthorized means valid authentication credentials are missing or invalid; HTTP 403 Forbidden means the user is successfully authenticated but lacks the required roles or permissions to access the resource.',
